id	1
unit	"Foundations"
title	"Vietnamese sounds and spelling"
description	"A guided 45–60 minute first lesson: understand Vietnamese syllables, vowel letters, tone marks and how to read carefully without using English spelling habits."
teacher_intro	"Welcome to your first Northern Vietnamese lesson. Set aside about 45–60 minutes. You do not need to memorise everything immediately. The method is simple: listen to the teacher, think about the answer, say it aloud before the students answer, and only then compare. Today we are learning how Vietnamese writing works. We are not trying to master every sound or all six tones yet; those will be trained more carefully in later lessons. Keep a notebook nearby if you want, but speak first."
background	"The modern Vietnamese writing system is chữ Quốc ngữ. It uses Latin letters, additional vowel letters and diacritics. A written space normally separates syllables. Several syllables can work together as one meaningful expression: học sinh means 'student' and giáo viên means 'teacher'. There are two different jobs that marks can do. First, vowel marks distinguish vowel letters such as a, ă and â; e and ê; o, ô and ơ; u and ư. Second, tone marks distinguish the six tones. For the vowel a, the written forms are a, á, à, ả, ã and ạ. Do not treat these marks as decoration. They are part of the spelling and meaning. This lesson introduces the system; detailed tone production and difficult vowel/consonant contrasts are practised in Lessons 2 and 3."
culture	"Chữ Quốc ngữ became the standard writing system used in modern Vietnamese education and everyday life. Earlier Vietnamese writing included chữ Nôm, and classical Chinese characters were also used in formal contexts. Today, Vietnamese is normally written with Quốc ngữ. In this course, examples and pronunciation guidance follow contemporary Northern Vietnamese, with a Hanoi reference accent. Regional varieties are valid; the course simply uses one consistent model so that you can build a reliable foundation."
dialogue	
0	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Before we begin, remember the method. I will give you a small piece of information, then ask you to build something. Pause the lesson if you need more time. Say your answer aloud before you reveal or read the student's answer."
1	
speaker	"STUDENT 1"
line	"So I should not just read the Vietnamese after you. I should try to produce it first."
2	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Exactly. Your attempt is the important part. A mistake is useful because it shows us what needs adjusting."
3	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"First idea: Vietnamese is organised into syllables. A syllable is a spoken beat. A written space usually marks the boundary between syllables."
4	
speaker	"STUDENT 2"
line	"Does that mean every space separates a completely separate idea?"
5	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"No. A phrase or compound meaning may contain several syllables. Look at học sinh. It means 'student', but it is written as two syllables: học and sinh."
6	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Your turn. If I give you the English meaning 'student', what two-syllable Vietnamese expression have we just learned? Pause and say it."
7	
speaker	"STUDENT 1"
line	"Học sinh."
8	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Good. Keep the two syllables distinct. Do not squash them into one English-style syllable."
9	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Now build another one. Giáo viên means 'teacher'. Listen to the two parts: giáo… viên. Say the complete expression."
10	
speaker	"STUDENT 2"
line	"Giáo viên."
11	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Good. Notice that the written space is still there. When you read Vietnamese, give each syllable its own clear shape."
12	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Let's test the idea. 'Vietnamese language' is tiếng Việt. How many written syllables can you see? Count them before answering."
13	
speaker	"STUDENT 1"
line	"Two: tiếng and Việt."
14	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Correct. The written space helps you see the two syllables."
15	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"A useful habit: when you meet a new expression, first identify the syllable boundaries. Then look at the consonants and vowel. Finally, notice the tone mark. Do not guess from the English translation."
16	
speaker	"STUDENT 2"
line	"So I should look at the whole written syllable, not just the first letter."
17	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Exactly. Now we need to understand the marks above and below the letters."
18	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Vietnamese uses familiar Latin letters, but some vowels have their own written forms. For example, a, ă and â are three distinct vowel letters. The small marks are not optional styling."
19	
speaker	"STUDENT 1"
line	"If I leave off the mark, am I still writing the same vowel?"
20	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"No. The vowel identity changes. We will practise the exact mouth positions in Lesson 3. For now, train your eyes to recognise that a, ă and â are different letters."
21	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"There are other groups too: e and ê; o, ô and ơ; u and ư. You do not need to master their pronunciation today. Just learn to see each spelling accurately."
22	
speaker	"STUDENT 2"
line	"I can see that o, ô and ơ are not interchangeable."
23	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Right. A useful reading routine is: identify the vowel letter exactly as written, including its mark. Never silently remove a diacritic."
24	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Now we come to a second job performed by marks: tone. Vietnamese has six tone categories in the standard teaching description. We will study how to hear and produce them in Lesson 2."
25	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"For now, look at the six written forms built on a: a, á, à, ả, ã, ạ. The unmarked form is one tone; each of the five marked forms represents another tone category."
26	
speaker	"STUDENT 1"
line	"So the accent marks are not just telling me which vowel to use. They can also tell me the tone."
27	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Exactly. Be careful not to confuse a vowel mark with a tone mark. In ă, the breve changes the vowel letter. In á, the acute accent marks the tone on the vowel a."
28	
speaker	"STUDENT 2"
line	"And ắ would combine the changed vowel with a tone mark?"
29	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Yes. A Vietnamese syllable can show both a vowel-shape mark and a tone mark. That is why careful spelling matters."
30	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Let's apply the routine to a familiar expression. Cảm ơn means 'thank you'. It has two written syllables. Look at the marks: cảm has a hỏi tone mark; ơn has the special vowel ơ."
31	
speaker	"STUDENT 1"
line	"So I should not write it as cam on without the marks."
32	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Correct. That would remove important spelling information. Write the complete forms, including the marks."
33	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Now try tiếng Việt. Both syllables carry tone marks. Read the spelling carefully from left to right, keeping the two syllables separate."
34	
speaker	"STUDENT 2"
line	"Tiếng Việt."
35	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Good. You are learning to notice the information before you speak."
36	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Let's review the three-step reading routine. One: find the syllable boundaries. Two: identify the exact vowel letters and their marks. Three: identify the tone mark. Only then produce the sound."
37	
speaker	"STUDENT 1"
line	"Syllables, vowel spelling, tone marks."
38	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Exactly. Now we will build short expressions from English prompts. Do not rush. Give yourself several seconds before checking the answer."
39	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Prompt one: 'student'. Think of the two syllables we learned. Say the Vietnamese expression now."
40	
speaker	"STUDENT 2"
line	"Học sinh."
41	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Prompt two: 'teacher'. Build it from giáo and viên."
42	
speaker	"STUDENT 1"
line	"Giáo viên."
43	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Prompt three: 'Vietnamese language'. Keep both syllables and their marks."
44	
speaker	"STUDENT 2"
line	"Tiếng Việt."
45	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Prompt four: 'thank you'. Notice the vowel ơ in the second syllable and the tone mark in the first."
46	
speaker	"STUDENT 1"
line	"Cảm ơn."
47	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Now combine what you know. 'Vietnamese student' is học sinh Việt Nam. Count the syllables: học, sinh, Việt, Nam. Four clear syllables."
48	
speaker	"STUDENT 2"
line	"Học sinh Việt Nam."
49	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Good. Do not turn this into one long blur. Keep the syllables clear and connected naturally."
50	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"A short cultural note: the writing system you are learning is chữ Quốc ngữ. It is the everyday writing system used in modern Vietnam. You will see it in signs, messages, books, menus and official documents."
51	
speaker	"STUDENT 1"
line	"And older Vietnamese writing included chữ Nôm?"
52	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Yes. Chữ Nôm was used historically, alongside classical Chinese characters in some formal settings. Modern everyday Vietnamese is written in Quốc ngữ."
53	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Before we finish, let's do a listening-and-noticing routine with a native Northern Vietnamese speaker. First listen without repeating. Then listen again and repeat one syllable at a time. Finally listen at natural speed."
54	
speaker	"NATIVE SPEAKER · NORTHERN VIETNAMESE"
line	"Recording script — careful pace: học… sinh. Pause. giáo… viên. Pause. tiếng… Việt. Pause. cảm… ơn. Pause. học… sinh… Việt… Nam."
55	
speaker	"NATIVE SPEAKER · NORTHERN VIETNAMESE"
line	"Recording script — natural pace: học sinh; giáo viên; tiếng Việt; cảm ơn; học sinh Việt Nam. Keep the syllables clear and use natural connected speech."
56	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Now repeat the same set without looking at the written answers. If one expression is difficult, return to its individual syllables and build it again."
57	
speaker	"STUDENT 2"
line	"I can see that the marks are part of the word, and the spaces help me divide the syllables."
58	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"That is the foundation. You do not need perfect pronunciation yet. Today you have learned how to read the written structure carefully. In Lesson 2, we will train the six Northern Vietnamese tones. In Lesson 3, we will work on vowel contrasts and difficult consonants."
59	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Final challenge: explain the three steps to yourself, then say học sinh, giáo viên, tiếng Việt, cảm ơn and học sinh Việt Nam without looking. Take your time. When you can do that, mark the lesson complete."
