unit	"Foundations"
title	"The six tones"
description	"Distinguish the six Northern Vietnamese tones and hear why tone changes meaning."
teacher_intro	"Today we train your ear and voice. Vietnamese has six tone categories in the standard teaching description. We will use one syllable, ma, to make the contrast easy to see. The goal is not to shout or add English stress; it is to shape the pitch and voice quality."
background	"The six tone names are ngang (level), sắc (rising), huyền (falling), hỏi (dipping), ngã (broken/rising with a glottal quality in Northern speech), and nặng (heavy, short and constricted). Actual pitch contours vary with sentence position and speaker."
culture	"This course uses contemporary Hanoi/Northern pronunciation. Northern and Southern varieties differ in the realisation of some tones and consonants. Neither is inherently more correct; they are regional varieties."
dialogue	
0	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Listen to the base syllable ma. Keep the vowel steady first. Now add a rising tone: má. Do not turn it into a loud English stress."
1	
speaker	"STUDENT 1"
line	"So tone is part of the syllable, not just emotion?"
2	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"Correct. A different tone can produce a different word. You must learn the vowel, consonants and tone together."
3	
speaker	"STUDENT 2"
line	"What about the two tones that sound a little interrupted?"
4	
speaker	"TEACHER · ENGLISH"
line	"In Hanoi speech, ngã often has a noticeable glottal interruption, while nặng is short, low and constricted. Listen to a native speaker rather than relying on a pitch drawing alone."
5	
speaker	"NATIVE SPEAKER · NORTHERN VIETNAMESE"
line	"Native recording slot: ma, má, mà, mả, mã, mạ; slow contrast set followed by natural-speed repetition. No recording is included yet."
