unit	"Foundations"
title	"Vowels and difficult consonants"
description	"Practise Vietnamese vowel contrasts and consonants that do not match English neatly."
teacher_intro	"Do not try to force every Vietnamese sound into an English category. We will practise the mouth shape and keep the final consonant short. Listen to the model, then imitate it."
background	"Vietnamese distinguishes several vowel qualities that English speakers may initially merge, including ă/â, e/ê, o/ô/ơ and u/ư. Final stops such as -p, -t, -c and -ch are unreleased: the mouth closes but no extra vowel follows."
culture	"Pronunciation varies regionally. This course follows a Northern reference accent, where d, gi and r are commonly pronounced with a z-like sound in Hanoi speech; other regions pronounce them differently."
